# os/debian.capabilities — the Debian-family capability declaration, NOBLE + TRIXIE
# tier (Core v5, #663/#667).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read (never sourced) by Core's zsh/02-capabilities.zsh into $_CORE_CAP, and by
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh, which is bash. bootstrap.sh links it to
# $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer.
#
# THE SOURCE of core/PORTING-MATRIX.md §"Package-manager commands", Debian/Ubuntu
# column (debian.kali.capabilities is the Kali one; every apt verb is identical) —
# the table is rendered from this file (dotfiles-core#686): edit here, then run
# `make gen-porting-matrix` in dotfiles-core.
# Validate with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/debian.capabilities
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THREE TARGETS, ONE MANIFEST — AND THE TIER SURVIVES INTO THIS FILE.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# This repo targets Ubuntu noble, Debian trixie and Kali rolling, already tiered in
# install/packages.txt by scripts/pkg-filter.sh's `only:` / `skip:` grammar. The apt
# VERBS are the same on all three, so almost all of this file is shared — but ONE key
# cannot be: Kali must not upgrade unattended (engagement boxes are updated by hand
# between ops, so a background upgrade breaks reproducibility mid-engagement).
#
# So Kali gets os/debian.kali.capabilities and bootstrap.sh relinks it, keyed on the
# SAME /etc/os-release ID ($OS_ID) that pkg-filter.sh tiers the package list on — so
# the declaration and the package list agree by construction rather than by coincidence.
# THIS file is noble and trixie. Keep the two in step by hand; only one key differs.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT — the reader keeps it. Notes go above.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=sudo apt-get update
# apt-get, not apt, everywhere in this file: `apt` prints a stability warning when its
# output is not a terminal and its interface is explicitly not stable for scripts.
# `full-upgrade`, not `upgrade`: this stack needs the removals `upgrade` refuses.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo apt-get full-upgrade
PKG_INSTALL=sudo apt-get install -y
PKG_REMOVE=sudo apt-get remove -y
PKG_SEARCH=apt-cache search
PKG_OWNS=dpkg -S
# SIMULATED against the lists already on disk: no root, no network, no mutation —
# which is what makes it safe to run from a once-a-day background nudge.
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=apt-get -s upgrade

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SCHEDULER=systemd
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# apt resolves against the lists in /var/lib/apt/lists, so refreshing is a real
# precondition and a failure ABORTS: an upgrade computed against an index that could
# not be refreshed is how a box half-applies.
PKG_UPGRADE_PRE=sudo apt-get update
PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=sudo apt-get install --only-upgrade
PKG_CLEANUP=sudo apt-get autoremove
PKG_ASSUME_YES=-y

# ── how `up` counts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `apt-get -s upgrade` prints `Inst <name> [<old>] (<new> ...)` for each pending
# package, among a lot of other simulation output. AN ERE CANNOT END IN A LITERAL
# SPACE — the reader trims trailing whitespace — so this is [[:space:]], never `^Inst `.
PKG_PENDING_MATCH=^Inst[[:space:]]
PKG_PENDING_FIELD=2

# ── the scheduled runner ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Noble and trixie are versioned, non-rolling releases whose security and maintenance
# updates are exactly what an unattended nightly is for — and on the headless SSH-only
# boxes this repo primarily targets, that nightly is often the only thing that applies
# them. It is also what these boxes do today: Core's untethered maint arm ran
# `apt-get update && full-upgrade -y && autoremove -y` here.
#
# KALI DOES NOT DECLARE THIS — see os/debian.kali.capabilities. The operator's
# MAINT_SYSTEM_UPGRADE=1 is still the FIRST of the two gates; this is only the second.
MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE=1

# ── optional tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A DECLARED LIST REPLACES Core's _CORE_DOCTOR_OPTIN — it does not add to it — so this
# re-states Core's nine and appends the two this family differs on.
#
# jj and ast-grep are `—²⁹` in PORTING-MATRIX.md's Debian/Ubuntu cells: not packaged on
# noble or trixie at all, and cargo-only (²¹) on Kali. Core's single list could not say
# "opt-in there, expected here", so both reported as expected-and-missing on every box —
# a degraded integration where nothing is actually wrong, which is the failure mode most
# likely to train an operator to ignore the report (#666).
#
# NOT dust, deliberately, however much it looks like the same case: noble has no
# `du-dust` package, so dust arrives OUT OF BAND under its plain name and is PRESENT.
# What differs there is the resolved binary name core-doctor displays, not whether the
# tool is expected — muting it here would hide a real absence.
TOOLS_OPTIN=lnav hyperfine watchexec shellcheck shfmt ouch git-absorb jnv gping jj ast-grep
